# Release and npm publish

Merging to `main` does **not** publish to npm. Only the **Publish** workflow runs, and only on a **version tag** push.

## How publish works

- Workflow: [`.github/workflows/publish.yml`](../.github/workflows/publish.yml)
- Trigger: `push` of tags matching `v*` (e.g. `v0.1.2`), not pushes to `main`
- Auth: npm **Trusted Publishing** (OIDC). `permissions.id-token: write` is required. There is no `NPM_TOKEN`.
- Command: `npm publish --provenance --access public`
- CI on `main` / PRs: [`.github/workflows/ci.yml`](../.github/workflows/ci.yml) — tests only

Do not replace the tag-based workflow with “publish on every push to `main`” unless the user explicitly asks. Tag releases keep npm in sync with intentional versions.

## Release checklist

1. Bump **`package.json`** `version`, **`src/version.ts`** (`VERSION`), and add a **`CHANGELOG.md`** section.
2. Merge to `main` (CI must pass).
3. Tag on the release commit; tag name (without `v`) must equal `package.json` version:
   ```sh
   git tag v0.1.3
   git push origin v0.1.3
   ```
4. Confirm the **Publish** workflow succeeded on GitHub Actions. npm may show “processing” for a few minutes after the first trusted publish.

If someone only merged without tagging, remind them to push the matching `v*` tag — that is why npm did not update.

## Version collisions

npm rejects a version that already exists. Always bump before tagging. `0.1.1` was tagged in git but never published after a failed pre–Trusted Publisher run; the next public release used a new version.
